@startuml sequence_queue_progress
!theme plain
' Compact layout (tuned per diagram — see README, 'Sequence-diagram layout').
hide footbox
title Queue Page – Live Progress & Control

participant "Queue Page\n(Streamlit)" as QP
participant "api_client\n(httpx)" as Client
participant "FastAPI\n/app.py" as API
participant "db.py\n(SQLite WAL)" as DB
participant "Runner\n(subprocess)" as Runner
participant "_progress.json" as Prog

== Poll loop (every ~2s via st.rerun) ==
loop Poll interval
    QP -> Client: GET /queue
    Client -> API: HTTP GET
    API -> DB: get_queue_state()
    DB --> API: {paused, running_id, queued_ids[]}
    API --> Client: QueueState
    Client --> QP: render control bar\n(pause/resume buttons)

    alt running experiment
        QP -> Client: GET /experiments/{id}/progress
        Client -> API: HTTP GET
        API -> DB: get_experiment(id) -> check status=running
        DB --> API: experiment record
        API -> API: read _progress.json from experiment dir
        API --> Client: ProgressSnapshot
        Client --> QP: render progress bar\n(run k/N, gen g/G)\nmetric cards (best, mean, wall time)

        QP -> Client: GET /experiments/{id}/stdout?offset=N
        Client -> API: HTTP GET
        API -> API: read _stdout.log from byte offset
        API --> Client: {text, next_offset, eof}
        Client --> QP: render stdout expander
        note over QP, API: custom "script" jobs emit no _progress.json —\nthe Queue shows stdout only (script prints its own progress)
    end

    QP -> Client: GET /experiments?limit=50
    Client -> API: HTTP GET
    API --> Client: ExperimentRecord[]
    Client --> QP: render all-experiments table
end

== Progress writes (trainer -> file) ==
Runner -> Trainer: train(config, progress_callback=...)
activate Trainer
loop each generation / progress step
    Trainer -> Runner: progress_callback(ProgressEvent)
    Runner -> Prog: atomic write\n(tmp + os.replace)
    note right of Prog: {run_index, total_runs,\nalgo, seed, unit,\ncurrent, total,\nbest_fitness, mean_fitness,\nwall_time_s}
end
deactivate Trainer

== Queue controls ==
QP -> QP: user clicks "Pause"
QP -> Client: POST /queue/pause
Client -> API: HTTP POST
API -> DB: set_paused(True)
DB --> API: ok
API --> Client: 200 {paused: true}
Client --> QP: update button state

QP -> QP: user clicks "Cancel" on running experiment
QP -> Client: POST /experiments/{id}/cancel
Client -> API: HTTP POST
API -> DB: get_experiment(id)
alt unknown id
    API --> Client: 404 {detail: "Unknown experiment ..."}
else already completed/failed/cancelled
    API --> Client: 409 {detail: "... already ... cannot be cancelled"}
else queued or running
    API -> API: dispatcher.cancel(id)
    note right of API: running + self._running_exp_id == id:\nterminate_group(proc) (taskkill /T on Windows;\nkillpg on POSIX) — kills the whole process tree.\nThe exp_id check guards against a stale/reassigned\nproc handle killing a different, just-started experiment.
    API -> DB: set_cancelled(id)
    note right of DB: no-op if the row already reached a terminal\nstatus
    DB --> API: ok
    API --> Client: 200 ExperimentRecord{status: "cancelled"}
end
Client --> QP: update experiment row

== Crash / restart recovery ==
note over API, DB: On dispatcher startup (service restart), any row still\nmarked 'running' means the *server* died mid-run, not the\nexperiment.
API -> DB: requeue_interrupted()
DB -> DB: running -> queued\n(clear pid / started_at / finished_at / progress_json,\nkeep original enqueue_seq)
note right of DB: the dispatcher's next poll picks it back up\nand restarts its sub-runs from the beginning\n(no population/optimizer/RNG checkpoint)

@enduml
